A: The problem in this scenario is that Pex doesn't support generics and doesn't operate on complex user-defined types correctly. This test case relies on complex behaviors of ArrayList that might not be straightforwardly translated into C# language constructs or simple API calls. 

To work around this issue, you could consider using a simpler structure like a List<T> if possible.

List<T>: It is a built-in collection of T which can grow as needed. 

Alternatively, you could reduce the complexity of your testing. For example, you could only test the basic functionalities (like adding an element, removing an element etc) and not the complex methods like LastIndexOf.

In conclusion, while you can use tools like Pex for quick checks, they may not always give the best coverage especially when you have custom classes or if the class behavior is complex to understand. In this case, manual unit tests or simpler test conditions might be a better approach. 

This situation is generally more suited for a static code analysis tool that can detect potential issues rather than a dynamic/mutation testing tool. 

