#### Code coverage
The analysis does not provide the complete coverage of code. However, it verifies a key aspect of the LastIndexOf method, which is to return the index of the last occurrence of an item in the ArrayList, or -1 if there is no such item. 

It should be noted that it may not be able to verify the contracts fully for types that are not simple objects or do not implement an appropriate .NET Framework interface. This method also does not support any exception conditions that might be present, so PEX is unable to fully confirm its correctness in those scenarios. 

In conclusion, this contract analysis is quite good, but the complete method cannot be confirmed by this analysis. We recommend you to continue to supplement with further unit tests to thoroughly verify the functionality of this method.

#### Further analysis
It's important to note that this analysis was performed based on the provided code without an understanding of the full design or the purpose of the 'arrList' data model. In a more realistic application, this method should ideally be accompanied by a range of unit tests that cover a wider range and permutations of expected inputs and outputs. 

To verify the array list contract, it would be beneficial to use other .NET method contracts testing tools such as Pex (Parameterized Code Coverage tool for .NET) which can be combined with the Visual Studio Testing tools like the MSTest and the NUnit Framework. Pex, in particular, is well-suited to the testing of complex method contracts. For more complex scenarios or methods that might throw exceptions, you may also need to add a verification of the expected exceptions in the unit tests. However, the effectiveness of automated contract testing with such sophisticated array list operations needs extensive real-world testing. Therefore, it's advisable to develop a wider range of test cases to uncover corner cases and potential issues. 

In general, it is always a good idea and best practice to thoroughly and extensively test your code with a variety of test scenarios, which can help to ensure its correctness and reliability. This includes testing both the expected behaviors and invalid inputs (i.e. negative testing). Automated unit testing and contract testing like the ones available in this analysis would be invaluable tools in the effective test automation workflow.

